# What the fuzzy finder walks, as data rather than as a list buried in the script.
#
# Two verbs, and everything after the first space is the path, so a path with a space in it
# needs no quoting. Paths are relative to the home directory, which is where the walk starts.
#
#   exclude  a tree the walk does not descend into
#   include  a tree walked in its own right, for reaching inside something excluded above.
#            A path that does not exist on this machine is skipped rather than being an error,
#            so one file can serve every machine and each uses the subset it has.
#
# The rule the cloud lines follow, because it is a category macOS defines rather than a list
# of this machine's junk. A picker must not traverse cloud provider storage. macOS puts all of
# it under exactly two fixed roots, identical on every Mac and independent of which accounts
# exist, so naming the pair is a rule rather than a patch.
#
# Library/CloudStorage is every third party provider, Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive, through
# the File Provider API. Library/Mobile Documents is iCloud. Only the first was here, and the
# missing twin is what made this picker unusable.
#
# What it costs to traverse one. Files macOS has evicted are dataless placeholders, and
# enumerating them blocks on the network rather than on the disk. Measured on one machine with
# Optimize Mac Storage on, the walk never finished, and the first two thousand rows alone took
# a minute and a half at zero percent CPU. Excluding the pair takes the whole scan to 0.27s.
#
# This is not one machine's misfortune, which is why it is written down here. Eviction follows
# disk pressure, so a machine where everything is downloaded today starts blocking later, and
# the picker appears to break on its own with nothing to point at.
#
# No detection is possible, checked rather than assumed. Evicted files sit on the same device
# and the same filesystem as everything else, so one file system check, a mount type test and
# --one-file-system are all blind to them. A deadline does not work either, since fd buffers
# when its output is a pipe and the blocked threads starve the stream. Spotlight answers
# without blocking, because it reads its own index, and is ten times slower and returns
# everything, so it needs this list anyway.
exclude Library/Caches
exclude Library/Containers
exclude Library/Group Containers
exclude Library/Application Support
exclude Library/Developer
exclude Library/CloudStorage
exclude Library/Mobile Documents
exclude node_modules
exclude .git
exclude .Trash
exclude .cache
exclude Applications

# The one thing wanted back out of iCloud, and the reason Mobile Documents was kept whole
# before. It is one container out of the hundred and ninety four there, so reaching it this way
# costs 0.025s instead of the whole tree. The path is the same on every Mac signed into this
# iCloud account, so it is account stable rather than machine specific.
#
# Its own files are evicted too, two thousand of them, and it is fast only because the walk
# reads directories and those are still materialised. If macOS ever evicts the directories as
# well this line is what will block, and the answer then is to drop it and reach notes through
# Obsidian instead of the finder.
include Library/Mobile Documents/iCloud~md~obsidian
